{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790322911000-vm-engineer-linker-m2-fixes-r2.glm-flash.report.md\nnl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc | sed -n '285,450p;765,875p;945,1090p'\"","aggregated_output":"# M2 gate-fix round 2 report (GLM-5.3-Flash subagent, verbatim extract)\n\nAll four gate findings fixed, touching only src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc\nand test/yin/vm/linker_test.cljc.\n\n1. P1 constant-key yin/def definitions — linker.cljc:301-316:\n   tree-definition-query extended with an or-join branch recognizing the\n   yin/def application shape the macro expander produces (macro.cljc:691,\n   yin/make-def; same recognition as macro.cljc definition-at): an\n   :application row whose operator row is [:variable yin/def], with\n   exactly two operands whose first is a :literal key, each binding\n   joined with its occurrence path. New test\n   a-constant-key-yin-def-application-is-a-definition (linker_test.cljc:1048)\n   over fixture yin-def-then-read (:133): the definition at [[3 0]], the\n   read discharged, only yin/def remaining an obligation the receiver\n   resolves through primitives.\n2. P1 prefix-ordering dominance — linker.cljc:320-329 and 421-432:\n   tree-application-query carries each application row's operand count,\n   and tree-application-sites records each site at its invocation\n   position: the row's path extended one step past its operands\n   (conj path [3 n)), which sorts after every operand step [3 i] and\n   before the parent's next step — the position where the walker\n   actually applies (ast_walker.cljc:232). A definition inside an\n   operand now dominates an application of the enclosing form. The AST\n   case added to a-definition-dominating-every-application-discharges-\n   a-body-occurrence (linker_test.cljc:1123): the tree verifies closed\n   with [] obligations; an-application-before-the-definition-retains-\n   the-obligation still refuses; its stale comment corrected.\n3. P1 decode-before-byte-cap bypass — linker.cljc:959-988: fetch-one\n   checks the byte length against the remaining budget before\n   jing/segment-bytes-match? and before any decode, and refuses\n   mismatched bytes with the address only — no decoded-value evidence.\n   The fetch docstring's step-2 order line updated. New test\n   a-hostile-byte-store-cannot-bypass-the-byte-cap (linker_test.cljc:953):\n   oversized mismatched bytes refuse :parts-limit/:max-bytes under a\n   tight cap and :address-mismatch with no :value under the defaults.\n   The two tests asserting the old decoded evidence\n   (local-storage-corruption-is-an-address-mismatch,\n   tree-with-one-corrupt-child-row-is-an-address-mismatch) now assert\n   the value's absence, per the prescribed fix.\n4. P2 :max-parts 0 quota gap — linker.cljc:1043-1045: fetch-parts\n   refuses a non-positive parts quota as :parts-limit naming :max-parts\n   and the root address before the worklist reads anything. New test\n   a-zero-parts-budget-refuses-the-root-unread (linker_test.cljc:998).\n\nNo finding conflicted with the spec (linker.md sections 4.1, 4.2, :413,\n:547).\n\nVerification (sequential, solo, all under mise):\n- cljstyle clean; clj-kondo 0 errors / 0 warnings on both files; pure\n  ASCII; every line <= 80 columns; no diagnostics; no commit/stage/\n  checkout/reset/stash (git state unchanged: the same four working-tree\n  modifications as at task start).\n- JVM full suite: 2,055 tests / 180,908 assertions / 0 failures\n  (baseline 2,052/180,898/0 plus the 3 new tests).\n- Node: 1,971 tests / 47,935 assertions / 0 failures.\n- Dart: 1,933 passed, \"All tests passed!\", exit 0.\n\nStatus: COMPLETE\n   285\t  [d u]\n   286\t  (neg? (path-order d u)))\n   287\t\n   288\t\n   289\t(def ^:private tree-occurrence-query\n   290\t  \"`vm/free-names`' free-name query over the row and occurrence\n   291\t   relations, extended to return each occurrence's path (section 4.1):\n   292\t   the same rules, one more find column.\"\n   293\t  '[:find ?name ?path\n   294\t    :in $ $occ % ?root\n   295\t    :where\n   296\t    [$occ ?root ?path ?v]\n   297\t    [?v :variable ?name]\n   298\t    (not (occ-bound? ?root ?path ?name))])\n   299\t\n   300\t\n   301\t(def ^:private tree-definition-query\n   302\t  \"The definition query behind `vm/ast-requirements` (section 4.1): the\n   303\t   `:vm/store-put` rows and the constant-key `yin/def` applications the\n   304\t   macro expander produces (an application of the `yin/def` variable\n   305\t   with exactly two operands, the first a literal), each binding joined\n   306\t   with its binding row's occurrence path.\"\n   307\t  '[:find ?key ?path\n   308\t    :in $ $occ ?root\n   309\t    :where\n   310\t    [$occ ?root ?path ?v]\n   311\t    (or-join [?v ?key]\n   312\t             [?v :vm/store-put ?key _]\n   313\t             (and [?v :application ?op ?operands _]\n   314\t                  [?op :variable yin/def]\n   315\t                  [(count ?operands) 2]\n   316\t                  [(nth ?operands 0) ?name-id]\n   317\t                  [?name-id :literal ?key]))])\n   318\t\n   319\t\n   320\t(def ^:private tree-application-query\n   321\t  \"The application-row query over the same relation (section 4.1): one\n   322\t   application site per `:application` row, carried with the row's\n   323\t   operand count -- the site is recorded where the application happens,\n   324\t   which is only after the operands have run.\"\n   325\t  '[:find ?path ?n\n   326\t    :in $ $occ ?root\n   327\t    :where\n   328\t    [$occ ?root ?path ?v]\n   329\t    [?v :application _ ?operands _]\n   330\t    [(count ?operands) ?n]])\n   331\t\n   332\t\n   333\t(defn- tree-row-at\n   334\t  \"The row `path` names from the tree's root, nil when the path leaves\n   335\t   the tree. A step is a `:node` slot's row position or a `[position i]`\n   336\t   pair into a `:nodes` slot's vector (section 6.1).\"\n   337\t  [{:keys [root rows]} path]\n   338\t  (loop [id root, steps (seq path)]\n   339\t    (if (nil? steps)\n   340\t      (get rows id)\n   341\t      (let [row (get rows id)\n   342\t            step (first steps)\n   343\t            child (when row\n   344\t                    (if (vector? step)\n   345\t                      (let [slot (nth row (nth step 0))]\n   346\t                        (when (vector? slot) (nth slot (nth step 1))))\n   347\t                      (nth row step)))]\n   348\t        (recur child (next steps))))))\n   349\t\n   350\t\n   351\t(defn- tree-enclosure\n   352\t  \"Whether the occurrence at `path` lies inside a lambda body, and\n   353\t   whether an `:if` branch or a lambda body encloses it -- section 4.1's\n   354\t   `:in-body?` and `:conditional?`. Each proper prefix of `path` names\n   355\t   the row the next step leaves: a `:lambda`'s body slot is 3, an `:if`'s\n   356\t   branches 3 and 4 (the section 2.3 grammar's own positions).\"\n   357\t  [tree path]\n   358\t  (loop [i 0, in-body? false, conditional? false]\n   359\t    (if (or (and in-body? conditional?) (>= i (count path)))\n   360\t      [in-body? conditional?]\n   361\t      (let [row (tree-row-at tree (subvec path 0 i))\n   362\t            tag (and row (nth row 1))\n   363\t            step (nth path i)]\n   364\t        (recur (inc i)\n   365\t               (or in-body? (and (= :lambda tag) (= 3 step)))\n   366\t               (or conditional?\n   367\t                   (and (= :lambda tag) (= 3 step))\n   368\t                   (and (= :if tag) (or (= 3 step) (= 4 step)))))))))\n   369\t\n   370\t\n   371\t(defn- tree-rows\n   372\t  \"The row and occurrence relations the tree scanners query, with the\n   373\t   tree's root.\"\n   374\t  [tree]\n   375\t  (let [root (:root tree)\n   376\t        db (query/relation (vals (:rows tree)))\n   377\t        occ (query/relation (vm/occurrences tree))]\n   378\t    {:root root, :db db, :occ occ}))\n   379\t\n   380\t\n   381\t(defn- tree-free-name-occurrences\n   382\t  \"Every free `:variable` occurrence of the tree `{:root root :rows {id\n   383\t   row}}` as a section 4.1 record `{:name sym :at [root path] :in-body?\n   384\t   b}`, in the tree's own path order: `vm/free-names`' occurrence rules\n   385\t   wrapped to carry each occurrence's path (section 4.1).\"\n   386\t  [tree]\n   387\t  (let [{:keys [root db occ]} (tree-rows tree)]\n   388\t    (->> (query/collect\n   389\t           (query/q tree-occurrence-query db occ\n   390\t                    vm/occurrence-rules root\n   391\t                    {:fns vm/occurrence-fns}))\n   392\t         (map (fn [[name path]]\n   393\t                (let [[in-body? _] (tree-enclosure tree path)]\n   394\t                  {:name name, :at [root path], :in-body? in-body?})))\n   395\t         (sort-by (comp second :at) path-order)\n   396\t         vec)))\n   397\t\n   398\t\n   399\t(defn- tree-definition-occurrences\n   400\t  \"Every constant store key the tree binds at module level as a section\n   401\t   4.1 record `{:name key :at [root path] :conditional? b}`: the\n   402\t   store-key query behind `vm/ast-requirements` (`:vm/store-put` rows),\n   403\t   each binding row carried with its occurrence path and enclosing tags\n   404\t   (section 4.1).\"\n   405\t  [tree]\n   406\t  (let [{:keys [root db occ]} (tree-rows tree)]\n   407\t    (->> (query/collect\n   408\t           (query/q tree-definition-query db occ root))\n   409\t         (map (fn [[key path]]\n   410\t                (let [[_ conditional?] (tree-enclosure tree path)]\n   411\t                  {:name key, :at [root path],\n   412\t                   :conditional? conditional?})))\n   413\t         (sort-by (comp second :at) path-order)\n   414\t         vec)))\n   415\t\n   416\t\n   417\t(defn- tree-application-sites\n   418\t  \"Every application row of the tree as a section 4.1 record `{:at\n   419\t   [root path]}` -- one application site at its invocation position: the\n   420\t   row's own path extended one step past its operands, the position at\n   421\t   which the walker applies the operator after evaluating them\n   422\t   (section 4.1), so a definition inside an operand dominates an\n   423\t   application of the enclosing form.\"\n   424\t  [tree]\n   425\t  (let [{:keys [root db occ]} (tree-rows tree)]\n   426\t    (->> (query/collect\n   427\t           (query/q tree-application-query db occ root))\n   428\t         (map (fn [[path n]]\n   429\t                {:at [root (conj path [3 n])]}))\n   430\t         (sort-by (comp second :at) path-order)\n   431\t         vec)))\n   432\t\n   433\t\n   434\t(defn- stack-free-occurrences\n   435\t  \"Every `:load-free` occurrence of a canonical stack instruction vector\n   436\t   as a section 4.1 record `{:name sym :at pc :in-body? b}`, in pc\n   437\t   order. Over a vector whose closure body spans the scope walk cannot\n   438\t   read, every `:load-free` name degrades to a record with no position\n   439\t   (section 4.1).\"\n   440\t  [v]\n   441\t  (if-let [spans (safe-layout v)]\n   442\t    (vec (keep-indexed\n   443\t           (fn [pc t]\n   444\t             (when (and (vector? t) (= :load-free (nth t 0)))\n   445\t               {:name (nth t 1), :at pc,\n   446\t                :in-body? (in-body-pc? spans pc)}))\n   447\t           v))\n   448\t    (degraded-occurrences (stack-free-names v))))\n   449\t\n   450\t\n   765\t                (refused :shadowed-free\n   766\t                         {:name (obligation-name obligation)})\n   767\t                (not (resolvable? receiver obligation))\n   768\t                (refused :unresolved-free\n   769\t                         {:name (obligation-name obligation)})))\n   770\t        obligations))\n   771\t\n   772\t\n   773\t;; =============================================================================\n   774\t;; Step 5a: the occurrence/definition join (section 4.2)\n   775\t;; =============================================================================\n   776\t\n   777\t(defn- precedes?\n   778\t  \"True when position `d` executes before position `u` under the\n   779\t   position's own order (section 4.1): a pc for the vector formats, the\n   780\t   tree's slot order for an occurrence path `[root path]`. Positions of\n   781\t   different shapes, or of different roots, never discharge each other.\"\n   782\t  [d u]\n   783\t  (cond\n   784\t    (and (integer? d) (integer? u)) (< d u)\n   785\t    (and (vector? d) (vector? u)\n   786\t         (= 2 (count d)) (= 2 (count u))\n   787\t         (= (nth d 0) (nth u 0)))\n   788\t    (path-before? (nth d 1) (nth u 1))\n   789\t    :else false))\n   790\t\n   791\t\n   792\t(defn- scanned\n   793\t  \"The records `slot`'s scanner yields for `value`, or nil when the slot\n   794\t   is missing or the scanner cannot run -- step 5a then falls under the\n   795\t   conservative degradation (section 4.1).\"\n   796\t  [format slot value]\n   797\t  (try (when-let [f (get format slot)] (f value))\n   798\t       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ nil)))\n   799\t\n   800\t\n   801\t(defn- undischarged\n   802\t  \"Step 5a's join: the free-name occurrences the scanners yielded,\n   803\t   against the definitions and the application sites. A definition\n   804\t   discharges an occurrence by defined-before-use under control-flow\n   805\t   dominance -- an unconditional definition whose position precedes the\n   806\t   occurrence's (section 4.1's order rules). A main-sequence occurrence\n   807\t   whose every definition is later is `:use-before-definition`; one\n   808\t   whose only definitions are conditional and earlier keeps its\n   809\t   obligation. A lambda-body occurrence is discharged only by an\n   810\t   unconditional definition that precedes every application site; an\n   811\t   image with no application site discharges a body occurrence against\n   812\t   every unconditional definition; with no usable application positions\n   813\t   nothing inside a body is discharged at all. A position the scanners\n   814\t   could not compute retains its occurrence and never discharges.\n   815\t   Returns `{:obligations [...]}` -- the retained records, in scan\n   816\t   order -- or a `:use-before-definition` refusal naming the\n   817\t   occurrence.\"\n   818\t  [occurrences definitions applications]\n   819\t  (let [sites (vec applications)\n   820\t        usable-sites? (and (sequential? applications)\n   821\t                           (every? (fn [s] (some? (:at s))) sites))\n   822\t        by-name (group-by :name definitions)\n   823\t        unconditional (fn [defs]\n   824\t                        (filter (fn [d]\n   825\t                                  (and (some? (:at d))\n   826\t                                       (false? (:conditional? d))))\n   827\t                                defs))\n   828\t        verdict\n   829\t        (fn [occ]\n   830\t          (let [at (:at occ)]\n   831\t            (if (nil? at)\n   832\t              :retained\n   833\t              (let [defs (get by-name (:name occ))]\n   834\t                (if-not (:in-body? occ)\n   835\t                  (let [dominating (filter (fn [d]\n   836\t                                             (precedes? (:at d) at))\n   837\t                                           (unconditional defs))]\n   838\t                    (cond\n   839\t                      (seq dominating) :discharged\n   840\t                      (and (seq defs)\n   841\t                           (every? (fn [d]\n   842\t                                     (and (some? (:at d))\n   843\t                                          (precedes? at (:at d))))\n   844\t                                   defs))\n   845\t                      :use-before-definition\n   846\t                      :else :retained))\n   847\t                  (cond\n   848\t                    (not usable-sites?) :retained\n   849\t                    (empty? sites)\n   850\t                    (if (seq (unconditional defs))\n   851\t                      :discharged\n   852\t                      :retained)\n   853\t                    :else\n   854\t                    (if (seq (filter (fn [d]\n   855\t                                       (every? (fn [s]\n   856\t                                                 (precedes? (:at d)\n   857\t                                                            (:at s)))\n   858\t                                               sites))\n   859\t                                     (unconditional defs)))\n   860\t                      :discharged\n   861\t                      :retained)))))))]\n   862\t    (reduce (fn [acc occ]\n   863\t              (case (verdict occ)\n   864\t                :discharged acc\n   865\t                :retained (update acc :obligations conj occ)\n   866\t                :use-before-definition\n   867\t                (reduced {:obligations []\n   868\t                          :defect (refused :use-before-definition\n   869\t                                           {:name (:name occ),\n   870\t                                            :at (:at occ)})})))\n   871\t            {:obligations []}\n   872\t            occurrences)))\n   873\t\n   874\t\n   875\t;; =============================================================================\n   945\t\n   946\t\n   947\t(defn- validation-defect\n   948\t  [format value]\n   949\t  (try ((:validate-fn format) value)\n   950\t       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n   951\t         {:rule :validator-refused})))\n   952\t\n   953\t\n   954\t(defn- fetch-one\n   955\t  \"One worklist visit: read `address` raw, check its size against the\n   956\t   byte `budget` remaining before hashing or decoding anything, verify\n   957\t   it against the address it was requested at, then validate it\n   958\t   row-locally -- in that order, so an oversized row is refused before\n   959\t   its bytes are hashed or decoded, mismatched bytes are never decoded\n   960\t   for evidence, and a malformed row is refused before anything it\n   961\t   references is decoded or enqueued. Returns `{:value v :bytes n}` or\n   962\t   a refusal.\"\n   963\t  [handle format identity address budget]\n   964\t  (let [bs (read-address handle address)]\n   965\t    (cond\n   966\t      (identical? missing bs)\n   967\t      (refused :absent {:address address})\n   968\t\n   969\t      (and budget (> (byte-count bs) budget))\n   970\t      (refused :parts-limit {:bound :max-bytes, :address address})\n   971\t\n   972\t      (not (jing/segment-bytes-match? address bs))\n   973\t      (refused :address-mismatch {:address address})\n   974\t\n   975\t      :else\n   976\t      (let [value (decoded bs)]\n   977\t        (if (nil? value)\n   978\t          (refused :address-mismatch {:address address, :value nil})\n   979\t          (if-let [defect (row-defect format value)]\n   980\t            (refused :descriptor-defect\n   981\t                     {:identity identity, :address address,\n   982\t                      :defect defect})\n   983\t            {:value value, :bytes (byte-count bs)}))))))\n   984\t\n   985\t\n   986\t(def default-bounds\n   987\t  \"The safe finite composition bounds a fetch falls back to where the\n   988\t   caller supplies none (section 4.2 step 2): generous for every honest\n   989\t   image the compositions hold, finite for a hostile one, so no fetch\n   990\t   path walks an unbounded worklist.\"\n   991\t  {:max-parts 4096, :max-depth 256, :max-bytes 16777216})\n   992\t\n   993\t\n   994\t(defn- bounded\n   995\t  \"The composition's `bounds` with every bound it left nil (or never\n   996\t   named) taking `default-bounds`.\"\n   997\t  [bounds]\n   998\t  (into default-bounds\n   999\t        (map (fn [[k v]] [k (or v (get default-bounds k))]))\n  1000\t        (or bounds {})))\n  1001\t\n  1002\t\n  1003\t(defn- enqueue-children\n  1004\t  \"The children of one fetched part that are not already enqueued, each\n  1005\t   paired with its depth, up to the parts budget: the first child beyond\n  1006\t   the remaining `max-parts` is refused `:parts-limit`, naming the bound\n  1007\t   and its own address, before it is fetched (section 4.2). Returns\n  1008\t   `{:queue [[address depth] ...] :fresh [address ...]}` or a refusal.\"\n  1009\t  [child-addrs enqueued depth max-parts]\n  1010\t  (loop [[child & more] (filter (fn [a] (not (contains? enqueued a)))\n  1011\t                                (distinct child-addrs))\n  1012\t         n (count enqueued)\n  1013\t         acc []]\n  1014\t    (cond\n  1015\t      (nil? child)\n  1016\t      {:queue (mapv (fn [a] [a (inc depth)]) acc), :fresh acc}\n  1017\t\n  1018\t      (>= n max-parts)\n  1019\t      (refused :parts-limit {:bound :max-parts, :address child})\n  1020\t\n  1021\t      :else\n  1022\t      (recur more (inc n) (conj acc child)))))\n  1023\t\n  1024\t\n  1025\t(defn- fetch-parts\n  1026\t  \"Step 2: the bounded breadth-first worklist from `root-address`\n  1027\t   (section 4.2). `:parts-fn` names the further addresses a fetched\n  1028\t   payload references; the walk enqueues them behind the current one,\n  1029\t   verifies each the same way, and visits each address once. `bounds` is\n  1030\t   the composition's `{:max-parts n :max-depth d :max-bytes b}`, every\n  1031\t   bound finite (`bounded`); exceeding one is `:parts-limit` naming the\n  1032\t   bound and the address at which it was hit. The parts budget is\n  1033\t   enforced at admission and at enqueue time -- a non-positive quota\n  1034\t   refuses the root before it is read, a child beyond the remaining\n  1035\t   budget is refused before it is fetched -- and the byte budget is\n  1036\t   checked before a payload is hashed or decoded. Returns\n  1037\t   `{:parts {address body} :multi? boolean}` or a refusal. `:multi?` is\n  1038\t   the record's own answer to whether the format has parts at all: a\n  1039\t   single-payload format's `:parts-fn` returns nil, a multi-part\n  1040\t   format's a sequence, possibly empty for a leaf row.\"\n  1041\t  [handle format identity root-address bounds]\n  1042\t  (let [{:keys [max-parts max-depth max-bytes]} (bounded bounds)]\n  1043\t    (if-not (pos? max-parts)\n  1044\t      (refused :parts-limit {:bound :max-parts, :address root-address})\n  1045\t      (loop [queue [[root-address 0]]\n  1046\t             index 0\n  1047\t             parts {}\n  1048\t             bytes 0\n  1049\t             enqueued #{root-address}\n  1050\t             multi? nil]\n  1051\t        (if (= index (count queue))\n  1052\t          {:parts parts, :multi? (boolean multi?)}\n  1053\t          (let [[address depth] (nth queue index)]\n  1054\t            (cond\n  1055\t              (and max-depth (> depth max-depth))\n  1056\t              (refused :parts-limit {:bound :max-depth, :address address})\n  1057\t\n  1058\t              :else\n  1059\t              (let [res (fetch-one handle format identity address\n  1060\t                                   (when max-bytes (- max-bytes bytes)))]\n  1061\t                (if (refused? res)\n  1062\t                  res\n  1063\t                  (let [value (:value res)\n  1064\t                        bytes' (+ bytes (:bytes res))\n  1065\t                        child-addrs ((:parts-fn format) value)\n  1066\t                        multi?' (if (nil? multi?)\n  1067\t                                  (not (nil? child-addrs))\n  1068\t                                  multi?)\n  1069\t                        enqueued' (enqueue-children child-addrs enqueued\n  1070\t                                                    depth max-parts)]\n  1071\t                    (if (refused? enqueued')\n  1072\t                      enqueued'\n  1073\t                      (recur (into queue (:queue enqueued'))\n  1074\t                             (inc index)\n  1075\t                             (assoc parts address value)\n  1076\t                             bytes'\n  1077\t                             (into enqueued (:fresh enqueued'))\n  1078\t                             multi?'))))))))))))\n  1079\t\n  1080\t\n  1081\t(defn fetch\n  1082\t  \"Fetches and verifies an image by its identity using handle, index, and\n  1083\t   format record; one surface for all four formats. Returns the verified\n  1084\t   image `{:status :ok ...}` or a qualified refusal.\n  1085\t\n  1086\t   `handle` is any DaoJing content handle; `index` is a map or function\n  1087\t   from identity to Jing address -- the identity function itself for the\n  1088\t   two storage-derived formats, whose identity is its own address\n  1089\t   (section 3); `format` is a format record; `identity` is H or R, a\n  1090\t   root row id, or a vector address. `receiver` is the receiving VM's\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
