That\n  1387\t  fallback was a forging vector: `engine/gensym` mints resource\n  1388\t  keys from the VM's counter (`:stream-0`, `:cursor-1`), the ids\n  1389\t  are predictable, and a linked module could emit\n  1390\t  `[:store-get :stream-0]` and read a host handle no publisher\n  1391\t  handed it. The split is therefore physical, not conventional:\n  1392\t  stream handles, cursor cells, and the FFI pair live in a private\n  1393\t  `:resources` table in VM state; the engine's own machinery\n  1394\t  (wait-set resolution, handle creation and polling, resume) reads\n  1395\t  and writes only that table; and no user store instruction and no\n  1396\t  `resolve-var` step consults it, at top level or inside a module\n  1397\t  closure. The ids may stay predictable -- they name nothing a\n  1398\t  store instruction can reach. This amends the store model UCF\n  1399\t  section 7.6.2 itself states -- that section opens by listing what\n  1400\t  the store S holds (listed with the other amendments in section\n  1401\t  11, item 12; r8's citation of a \"UCF 4.1\" named no such section,\n  1402\t  corrected r10): a migration slice still carries resource\n  1403\t  cells, remapped at resume, but beside the program store, never\n  1404\t  inside it.\n  1405\t\n  1406\t  **Resource lowering (r9).** The continuation format's own decode\n  1407\t  rules move with the split, or the private table is bypassed at\n  1408\t  every boundary crossing: UCF section 7.5.1's table decodes a\n  1409\t  `:yin.k/stream` marker to \"an attached handle, under a fresh\n  1410\t  store key\" and a `:yin.k/cursor-ref` to \"a store cursor entry\",\n  1411\t  and section 7.5.3 lowers each logical cell to \"a fresh store\n  1412\t  cursor entry\". From r9 those decode targets are the private\n  1413\t  table, as an amendment to UCF sections 7.5.1 and 7.5.3 (added to\n  1414\t  the list in section 11, item 12): lowering installs an attached\n  1415\t  handle under a fresh **resource id** in `:resources`, lowers a\n29:  contract stamp, safepoints, pending waits, primitive profiles\n298:  name, or a call of a primitive that receives a function argument,\n309:  against `:store-put`, `:load-free` against the `yin/def` call\n350:                 name bound in free env or store    -> :shadowed-free\n417:     `:store-put` operands, `yin/def` applications with a constant key),\n477:       (`yin.vm.engine`) falls through to primitives and the module\n488:     `:yin.module/primitives` becomes\n489:     `{:name n :kind :primitive :profile addr}`; a name exported by a\n507:     a `:primitive` obligation is discharged only by a primitive of\n510:     free env or store is `:shadowed-free`; anything else is\n553:| `:shadowed-free`        | 5b     | an obligation's name is bound by the     |\n651:call opcodes, including calls of imported names and of primitives\n680:including calls of imported names and function-argument primitives.\n891:`yin.vm/primitives` binds `require` to an effectful primitive that\n1320:  and fall through to its primitives and module registry -- where\n1350:    receiver binding of the same name can neither supply nor shadow\n1354:    through it first and the receiver's free env, primitives, and\n1378:    slot, which is the publisher's semantics, not a shadow.\n1536: :yin.module/primitives  {'println :yin.k.pp/sha256-...}       ; profile\n1626:  manifest address the publisher linked against; `:yin.module/primitives`\n1719:    function and implements no cryptographic primitive itself (a host\n1785:must carry a profile in the shape of `yin.vm/primitive-profiles`, and\n1797:`:yin.module/primitives` by profile address.\n1916:same-named primitive of different profile at the receiver is\n1917:`:unresolved-free`; a shadowed export name in the requiring program's\n1918:store is `:shadowed-free`; a free name the manifest never declares is\n1926:shadows it, and a write from one export is visible to the next\n2007:          yin.vm (validate-rows, free-names, pr