e-resource rule below. The context\n  1362\t    threads through calls: a frame records the caller's active\n  1363\t    `:store-of` (or none), applying a closure makes that closure's\n  1364\t    `:store-of` active, and returning restores the frame's, so a\n  1365\t    module closure calling another module's closure routes each\n  1366\t    body's reads and writes to its own module. A tail call that\n  1367\t    empties the continuation -- the closure's body was the whole\n  1368\t    computation -- clears the active context with the last frame,\n  1369\t    and the VM admits new top-level input only with no active\n  1370\t    context, which `yin.repl`'s append asserts, so input after a\n  1371\t    module closure applied never routes through a stale module\n  1372\t    store. Sibling exports in one task share one store instance,\n  1373\t    so a write one makes is visible to the next; each task holds\n  1374\t    its own instances, so writes never cross tasks. A declared\n  1375\t    obligation the store does not hold falls through to the\n  1376\t    receiver's bindings exactly as step 5b checked them; a\n  1377\t    conditional definition that ran overwrites the module's own\n  1378\t    slot, which is the publisher's semantics, not a shadow.\n  1379\t  - *In the child.* The same discipline holds transitively: a\n    54\t(defn resolve-var\n    55\t  \"Look up a variable name: env -> store -> primitives -> module registry.\n    56\t\n    57\t   The registry is a value supplied by the composition, not a global.\"\n    58\t  [env store primitives registry name]\n    59\t  (if-let [pair (find env name)]\n    60\t    (val pair)\n    61\t    (if-let [pair (find store name)]\n    62\t      (val pair)\n    63\t      (if-let [pair (find primitives name)]\n    64\t        (vm/primitive-function (val pair))\n    65\t        (if-let [resolved (when (namespace name)\n    66\t                            (module/resolve-module\n    67\t                              registry\n